%N A400972 Irregular triangle read by rows: T(m,j) is the number of nonnegative integers k for which (2j-1)/2^m + k/2 is not a fusible number, for m >= 2 and 1 <= j <= 2^(m-2).
%C A400972 A fuse burns for exactly one minute, at an irregular rate. Either end of a fuse may be lit, but only at time 0 or at a moment when some fuse burns out, and no fuse is put out. The times, in minutes, at which a fuse can burn out, together with 0, are the fusible numbers of Erickson (see A188545): the smallest set F that contains 0 and contains (x+y+1)/2 whenever x and y are in F and |x-y| < 1 (a fuse whose ends are lit at the times x and y burns out at the time (x+y+1)/2). Taking y = x shows that x + 1/2 is in F whenever x is.
%C A400972 Row m runs through the fractions r = (2j-1)/2^m between 0 and 1/2 whose denominator is exactly 2^m. Since x + 1/2 is fusible whenever x is, the numbers r + k/2 that are not fusible are r, r + 1/2, ..., r + (T-1)/2 for some T = T(m,j), which is finite (see the Formula section). For example, T(4,1) = 3, since 1/16, 9/16 and 17/16 are not fusible but 25/16 is. Row m begins at n = 2^(m-2).
%C A400972 This triangle refines A401905: (A401905(m)-1)/2^m is the largest multiple of 1/2^m that is not fusible, and it is the largest of the 2^(m-2) numbers r + (T(m,j)-1)/2 of row m, whereas row m records the largest number that is not fusible in each class r, r + 1/2, r + 1, ... separately. For example, in row 4 these numbers are 17/16, 11/16, 13/16 and 7/16, and A401905(4) = 18.
%C A400972 The values 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6 first occur at n = 1, 2, 4, 16, 512 and 2^50 (the first terms of rows 2, 3, 4, 6, 11 and 52), and a(n) <= 6 for all n < 2^c, where the integer c = V_11(1) of the Formula section is larger than 2^^^^^^^^^16 (Knuth's up-arrow notation with nine arrows: one arrow means exponentiation, and each further arrow iterates the preceding operation). Every positive integer occurs, and for each K the proportion of n <= N with a(n) <= K tends to 0 as N -> oo.
%C A400972 The slow growth comes from the gaps between consecutive fusible numbers: a number with denominator 2^m that is not fusible lies strictly inside such a gap, whose length is then at least 2^(1-m). The least fusible number greater than 5/2 is 5/2 + 2^(-51), which gives the first 6 (it is not 5/2 + 2^(-31): the recursion M of Erickson, Nivasch and Xu only bounds the gaps from above; see A383922), and no gap that begins at 3 or later is longer than 2^(-1-c). Peano arithmetic cannot prove that the terms are unbounded; this follows from Theorems 1.2 and 6.1 of Erickson, Nivasch and Xu, as for A401905(m)/2^m in A401905.
%C A400972 The statements of this entry that are not cited from the literature are proved in the notes file (see the Links section). The gap bounds are obtained from the "meek" fusible numbers, a subset of F defined by Erickson, Nivasch and Xu (their Section 7): the notes prove the recurrences that those authors state without proof (their Lemma 7.2), and prove that every fusible number <= 5/2 is meek, on which the last bracket of the rule in the Formula section and the first 6 rest. Whether every fusible number is meek is their Conjecture 7.1; if it is true, then the first 7 is at n = 2^c.
%H A400972 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A400972/b400972.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..8191</a>
%H A400972 Jeff Erickson, Gabriel Nivasch and Junyan Xu, <a href="https://doi.org/10.46298/lmcs-18(3:6)2022">Fusible numbers and Peano Arithmetic</a>, Logical Methods in Computer Science, Vol. 18, No. 3 (2022), 6:1-6:26; arXiv:<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.14342">2003.14342</a> [cs.LO], 2020-2022.
%H A400972 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A400972/a400972.txt">Notes, proofs and sources</a>.
%H A400972 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A400972/a400972_1.txt">Rust program</a>.
%H A400972 Jianing Song, <a href="/A383922/a383922.pdf">Notes on A383922</a>, 2025.
%H A400972 Junyan Xu, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1202.5614">Survey on Fusible Numbers</a>, arXiv:1202.5614 [math.CO], 2012.
%H A400972 Junyan Xu, <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/alreadydone/fusible/master/computation.pdf">Computations of margins of fusible numbers</a>, unpublished note, 2015.
%F A400972 a(n) = T(m,j) with m = floor(log_2(n)) + 2 and j = n - 2^(m-2) + 1.
%F A400972 A401905(m) = 1 + max_{1 <= j <= 2^(m-2)} ((2*j-1) + (T(m,j)-1)*2^(m-1)) for m >= 2.
%F A400972 max_{1 <= j <= 2^(m-2)} T(m,j) = 1 + floor((A401905(m)-2)/2^(m-1)) for m >= 2.
%F A400972 1 <= T(m,j) <= m-1, and T(m,j) = 1 if and only if j = 2^(m-2).
%F A400972 T(m+1,1) - T(m,1) is 0 or 1.
%F A400972 Let u = 1/2 - (2*j-1)/2^m = 2^(-e(1)) + 2^(-e(2)) + ... + 2^(-e(s)) with e(1) < e(2) < ... < e(s) = m (the binary expansion of u), and let p = e(1). Put I(0) = 2 and I(q+1) = e(I(q)) + 3 + 2*q - p for 0 <= q < p, where e(t) = oo for t > s, so that I(q+1) = oo if I(q) > s. Then, in every row, min(T(m,j), 5) = 1 + [s >= 2] + [s >= 3] + [s >= p+3] + [s >= I(p)+2], where [ ] is the Iverson bracket. The first three brackets express the binary description of the fusible numbers below 2 that appears in Xu's 2015 note and in Song's notes on A383922.
%F A400972 T(m,j) <= 5 for m <= 52, except that T(52,1) = 6 (so that A401905(52) = 5*2^51 + 2). So the preceding formula gives a(n) for all n < 2^51 except n = 2^50.
%F A400972 Row m contains m-2 terms equal to 2 and, for m >= 3, Fibonacci(m+2) - 2*m + 1 = A001891(m-3) terms equal to 3.
%F A400972 The sum of row m is the number of positive fractions with denominator exactly 2^m that are not fusible. It is equal to A008466(m) = 2^m - Fibonacci(m+2) for 2 <= m <= 10 and larger for m >= 11; the row sums begin 1, 3, 8, 19, 43, 94, 201, 423, 880, 1816.
%F A400972 The constant c in the comments is V_11(1), where V is defined by the following recurrences of Erickson, Nivasch and Xu (their Section 7.2). For k >= 1, L_1(k) = Y_1(k) = k+2, V_1(k) = 2*k+8 and, for i >= 2, with f^(t) denoting the t-th iterate of f (f^(0)(x) = x):
%F A400972   L_i(k) = L_(i-1)^(i+k)(1), Y_i(k) = 1 + Sum_{t=0..i+k-1} Y_(i-1)(L_(i-1)^(t)(1)),
%F A400972   J_i(k) = L_(i-1)^(i+k+1)(1), U_i(k) = 1 + Sum_{t=0..i+k} Y_(i-1)(L_(i-1)^(t)(1)), V_i(k) = U_i(k) + V_(i-1)(J_i(k)).
%F A400972 V_2(k) = k^2 + 12*k + 38. In the set of meek numbers (see Comments) the gaps after 5/2, 11/4 and 3 are 2^(-V_2(1)) = 2^(-51), 2^(-V_3(1)) = 2^(-48804) and 2^(-1-V_11(1)).
%e A400972 The first five rows are:
%e A400972   m = 2:  1
%e A400972   m = 3:  2, 1
%e A400972   m = 4:  3, 2, 2, 1
%e A400972   m = 5:  3, 3, 3, 2, 3, 2, 2, 1
%e A400972   m = 6:  4, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 2, 3, 3, 3, 2, 3, 2, 2, 1
%e A400972 The following numbers are fusible: 1/2 = (0+0+1)/2, 3/4 = (0+1/2+1)/2, 7/8 = (0+3/4+1)/2, 15/16 = (0+7/8+1)/2, 1 = (1/2+1/2+1)/2, 9/8 = (1/2+3/4+1)/2, 19/16 = (1/2+7/8+1)/2, 21/16 = (3/4+7/8+1)/2 and 25/16 = (1+9/8+1)/2. Below 1 the only fusible numbers are 0 and the numbers 1 - 1/2^t (t >= 1), and no fusible number lies strictly between 1 and 9/8. So rows 2 to 4 are obtained as follows:
%e A400972   m   r      not fusible            first fusible   T(m,j)
%e A400972   2   1/4    1/4                    3/4             1
%e A400972   3   1/8    1/8, 5/8               9/8             2
%e A400972   3   3/8    3/8                    7/8             1
%e A400972   4   1/16   1/16, 9/16, 17/16      25/16           3
%e A400972   4   3/16   3/16, 11/16            19/16           2
%e A400972   4   5/16   5/16, 13/16            21/16           2
%e A400972   4   7/16   7/16                   15/16           1
%e A400972 T(6,1) = 4 by the formula: u = 1/2 - 1/64 = 2^(-2) + 2^(-3) + 2^(-4) + 2^(-5) + 2^(-6), so e = (2,3,4,5,6), s = 5, p = 2, I(1) = e(2) + 3 + 0 - 2 = 4 and I(2) = e(4) + 3 + 2 - 2 = 8. The four brackets are [5 >= 2] = 1, [5 >= 3] = 1, [5 >= 5] = 1 and [5 >= 10] = 0, so T(6,1) = 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 0 = 4.
%e A400972 T(11,1) = 5: u = 1/2 - 1/2048 has s = 10 and e(t) = t+1, so again p = 2 and I(2) = 8, and the last bracket is [10 >= 10] = 1. So min(T(11,1), 5) = 5, and T(11,1) = 5 because T(m,j) <= 5 for m <= 51.
%o A400972 (Python)
%o A400972 # B[t] says whether t/2^R is fusible (0 <= t/2^R <= R/2). A fusible multiple
%o A400972 # of 1/2^R is obtained from fusible multiples of 1/2^R alone (see A401905).
%o A400972 R = 10
%o A400972 Q = 2**R
%o A400972 B = [False] * (R * Q // 2 + 1)
%o A400972 B[0] = True
%o A400972 for t in range(Q // 2, len(B)):
%o A400972     s = 2 * t - Q
%o A400972     B[t] = any(B[i] and B[s-i]
%o A400972                for i in range(max(0, s-t+1), min(t-1, s//2)+1))
%o A400972 for m in range(2, R+1):
%o A400972     for j in range(1, 2**(m-2)+1):
%o A400972         r = (2*j-1) * 2**(R-m)
%o A400972         print(next(k for k in range(1, m) if B[r+k*Q//2]))
%o A400972 (Python)
%o A400972 # Prints min(a(n), 5) for n = 1..N; this is a(n) for all n < 2**50.
%o A400972 N = 8191
%o A400972 for n in range(1, N + 1):
%o A400972     m = n.bit_length() + 1
%o A400972     j = n - 2**(m - 2) + 1
%o A400972     u = 2**(m - 1) - (2*j - 1)  # Numerator of 1/2 - r.
%o A400972     e = [t for t in range(2, m + 1) if u & 2**(m - t)]
%o A400972     s, p = len(e), e[0]
%o A400972     I = 2
%o A400972     for i in range(p):
%o A400972         if I > s:
%o A400972             break
%o A400972         I = e[I - 1] + 3 + 2*i - p
%o A400972     print(1 + (s >= 2) + (s >= 3) + (s >= p+3) + (s >= I+2))
%o A400972 (Rust) // See the Ferudun link "Rust program".
